        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
NASA has understood the educational benefits of using simulation during its history as demonstrated by its integration of simulation into multiple aviation disciplines to prepare its astronauts for space flight. The NASA Space Life Sciences Directorate (SLSD) at the Johnson Space Center (JSC) has observed these benefits along with recognizing the recent success of medical patient simulation in teaching terrestrial medicine. As a result, the SLSD has developed an infrastructure to use high-fidelity medical patient simulation as a teaching tool for astronaut crew medical officers (CMO) and flight surgeons (FS). In order to integrate medical patient simulation into medical training programs, the SLSD tasked the NSBRI with constructing a team that has the expertise and experience to develop medical patient simulation-based curricula. 
<p>
The NSBRI assembled diverse clinical and scientific personnel from both inside and outside the space medicine community to create the Medical Operation Support Team (MOST). Since its inception, the team has used its unique skill sets to develop eight medical patient simulation-based products for these cohorts per the direction of NASA Medical Operations Branch (MOB), the Astronaut Office, and the NASA Human Research Program (HRP). Overall, the MOST has used its expertise with their strong space medicine collaborations and NASA-JSC’s medical patient simulator infrastructure to develop robust space medicine training products. 
<p>
Specific Aims:<br>
1. Adapt Human Patient Simulator (HPS) physiologic scripting and modeling to space flight training. <br>

2. Develop an integrated, multimedia medical curriculum and web development to integrate with the HPS training sessions. <br>
3. Develop, evaluate, and validate space medical procedures. <br>
4. Maintain and expand the multidisciplinary team in conjunction with NASA-JSC's needs and requests. <br>
APPROACH:<br>
The methodology for the study is embedded in the results below.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The key findings of the aims and their impact are listed below. Please note that the following tasks were accomplished on a (mostly) volunteer/non-funded basis due to the decrease in project funding.
<p>
The Medical Operation Support Team (MOST) developed an experimental integrated medical simulation program to instruct astronauts and their ground-based flight surgeons how to mitigate medical issues related to space flight. The team conducted this experimental training for two cohorts.
<p>
Training was developed for the flight surgeons and astronaut crew medical officers (CMO) of the STS-118 Space Shuttle mission, per the request of the Lead CMO. The team developed curricula using medical patient simulators to train the CMOs how to mitigate space flight-related medical events while being remotely guided by their ground-based flight surgeons in Mission Control. The session was the first simulation exercise of its kind and is being reviewed by the Medical Operations Branch (MOB) at NASA-Johnson Space Center for possible integration into the Space Medicine Training flow. 
<p>
The MOST developed an emergency medicine scenario for an ‘Emergency Medical Drills’ session that was conducted during the 12th and 13th expeditions of the NASA Extreme Mission Operation (NEEMO) project. NEEMO consists of an underwater habitat that is supposed to mimic the isolation and constraints experience during space flight and, thus, this project acts as a good test-bed for researchers to evaluate procedures and equipment for possible use during space missions. The data collected by the MOST from these ‘Drills’ demonstrated that that having a physician as a CMO led to an expeditious identification and treatment of an unknown medical condition. As a result of this finding, the Medical Operations Branch (MOB) at NASA-Johnson Space Center is reviewing whether implementation of physician-astronauts as Lead CMO would be beneficial for long duration space missions (e.g. International Space Station, Moon, and Mars).
<p>
